  "problem": "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find all solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the second solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=-1$ and we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
